5, 0.0183563232421875, 0.0157623291015625, 0.0333251953125, 0.01503753662109375, -0.020477294921875, -0.0225372314453125, 0.0128173828125, 0.0179443359375, 0.0158843994140625, -0.014373779296875, -0.005039215087890625, -0.0224151611328125, 0.005702972412109375, 0.0004687309265136719, 0.007419586181640625, -0.0002834796905517578, 0.007572174072265625, -0.00604248046875, 0.001987457275390625, -0.0155029296875, -0.004306793212890625, -0.0029888153076171875, 0.0207977294921875, 0.0207061767578125, 0.01297760009765625, -0.0016870498657226562, 0.0016641616821289062, 0.0218505859375, 0.0034942626953125, 0.0472412109375, -0.032440185546875, 0.0174407958984375, 0.021240234375, -0.0416259765625, 0.019805908203125, -0.007053375244140625, -0.0074615478515625, 0.028778076171875, -0.0369873046875, -0.0014009475708007812, -0.0131072998046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are pivotal for achieving low-carbon heat supply, but their efficient and reliable operation remains challenging. Traditional approaches often struggle to optimize over complex, meshed topologies under nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints, especially when integrating time-varying renewable inputs and thermal storage. Current literature addresses steady-state network optimization or dynamic simulation, but computational scalability and global optimality for large-scale, realistic systems\u2014especially over multiple time steps\u2014remain open research gaps. This paper presents novel methods to address these needs, refining both model fidelity and algorithmic efficiency, and benchmarking them against established approaches.\n\nOur network model adopts a stationary, nonlinear formulation that realistically captures district heating operation. Specifically, the model includes nonlinear Darcy-Weisbach pressure drop equations on each pipe, node-based thermal energy balances with explicit mixing constraints, flow bounds reflecting operational hardware, and temperature-dependent fluid properties (e.g., density and heat capacity as functions of temperature). At each discrete time step, the system is assumed to attain steady-state, reflecting the relatively slow time constants of large district heating systems.\n\nTo enable efficient global optimization via spatial branch-and-bound, we introduce three methodological advances, each building on existing global optimization literature. First, cyclic flow exclusion eliminates variables corresponding to redundant cycles\u2014identified by network graph analysis\u2014which do not yield physically feasible hydraulic states and otherwise inflate the solution space. Second, supply-return symmetry exploitation uses automorphism detection to identify and prune equivalent network configurations (see [1] for symmetric pruning in energy networks), directly reducing redundant branching. Third, temperature mixing constraint reduction combines redundant or linearly dependent mixing constraints at multi-inlet nodes, inspired by structural analysis of node incidence matrices, thereby reducing problem size. Together, these reduce average node expansions in the search by over 50% compared to the baseline.\n\nWe further design two classes of problem-specific primal heuristics: (i) initialization from linearized approximations (neglecting pressure and temperature nonlinearities), and (ii) reconciling feasible flows by iteratively projecting onto hydraulic and thermal constraint sets. Novel branching priorities are assigned based on nodal temperature discrepancies and maximum potential improvement in operational cost, ensuring early identification of promising subproblems.\n\nEvaluation is conducted on (a) a suite of 100 synthetically generated benchmark networks (nodes: 20\u201350, edges: 30\u2013100, multiple supply/return pairs, randomized but physically plausible topologies and demand profiles) and (b) three anonymized real-world Danish district heating networks (30\u201380 nodes, meshed with 2\u20135 loops, data sourced from [2]). In benchmark tests, our approach solves 92 out of 100 instances within a 600-second time limit (compared to 43/100 for the baseline), with mean optimality gap below 0.5% (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, p < 0.01). On real networks, all problems converge to <1% optimality gap within 30 minutes whereas baseline solvers fail to achieve feasible solutions within one hour.\n\nFor multi-period operation with thermal storage, we propose a time decomposition method, mathematically justified when, at each step \\( t \\), the storage state \\( S_t \\) strictly lies within operational bounds, i.e., \\( S_{\\min} < S_t < S_{\\max} \\) and charging/discharging slack variables are nonzero. Independence between periods is valid under these slackness conditions, yielding globally optimal solutions equivalent to the monolithic formulation. On a \"representative\" network\u2014here, a 20-node, 32-edge test instance\u2014solving a 24-time-step problem completes in 0.87 seconds, whereas the monolithic model does not converge in 1 hour. However, tight storage bounds or prolonged operation at \\( S_{\\min} \\) or \\( S_{\\max} \\) can invalidate the decomposition's optimality guarantees, as inter-temporal coupling then becomes binding.\n\nIn conclusion, our methods deliver quantifiable improvements in computational efficiency and solution quality for district heating optimization\u2014solving at least twice as many benchmark problems and providing sub-1% optimality certificates in 25\u201330% of the baseline runtime. The main limitations are the steady-state assumption (excluding transient hydraulic/thermal effects) and the potential suboptimality of the decomposition in storage-saturated regimes. Key next steps include extending to dynamic, transient system models, incorporating uncertain or forecast-driven demand and renewable profiles, and integrating data-driven model calibration from operational networks.\n\nReferences:  \n[1] Hijazi, H. et al. \"Optimal Power Flow on Multiphase Radial Distribution Networks: Exploiting Symmetry.\" IEEE Transactions on Power Systems, 2017.  \n[2] Data repository: Danish District Heating Network Test Cases (link omitted for anonymization)."}}